{"qid":"Q730","names":["Republic of Suriname"],"title":"Republic of Suriname","endonym":{"native":"Republiek Suriname","lang":"nl"},"capsule":"Suriname gained independence from the Netherlands on 25 November 1975, the last Dutch colony on the South American mainland. The democratic government of Henck Arron was overthrown in the 1980 military coup led by Dési Bouterse, whose regime killed fifteen prominent opponents in the December Murders of 1982 and waged a counterinsurgency against the Jungle Commando Maroon uprising (1986–1992). A return to civilian rule in 1991 inaugurated an unstable democratic period; the economy remained heavily dependent on bauxite, gold, and oil exports, and Bouterse himself was controversially elected president in 2010 and 2015.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Suriname's international borders are mostly surveyed, but boundary disputes with Guyana (Corentyne River region) and French Guiana (Maroni River) remain nominally unresolved; the internal territory is precisely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Suriname","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suriname"},{"label":"Britannica: Suriname","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Suriname"},{"label":"Edward Dew, The Difficult Flowering of Surinam (Martinus Nijhoff, 1978)","url":"https://archive.org/details/difficultfloweri0000dewe"},{"label":"Gary Brana-Shute, 'Suriname: The Politics of Accommodation', in Latin American and Caribbean Politics (NACLA, 1990)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suriname#cite_note-Brana-Shute-1"}],"britannica":"place/Suriname","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Suriname","rulers":[